Exactly. Same structural language, two environmental species. And I wouldn't merely make the indoor one a cheaper version. Their surfaces and embedded functions should be deliberately optimized for radically different environments.

01 — SUNFORGE

Exterior / architectural / weather-facing

This is the one that lives outside indefinitely.

Its priorities are:

UV → rain → thermal cycling → humidity → wind → impact → corrosion → freeze/thaw → maintenance

The exterior skin should be the sacrificially replaceable interface rather than the structural core. That lets us make the underlying structural member enormously durable while allowing the weather-facing surface to be renewed decades later.

And this is where your SOLSHADE work feeds directly back into the material.

The exterior member could have standardized longitudinal zones for:

PV attachment

thermal harvesting

piezoelectric strain/vibration harvesting

rainwater routing

cable routing

sensor placement

mechanical attachment


So a SUNFORGE wall isn't just a wall.

It's potentially a distributed environmental-energy/sensing network.

Sun heats it → thermal differential becomes available.
Wind flexes it → strain/vibration becomes available.
Rain hits it → vibration/impact becomes available.
People walk on attached floors → mechanical energy becomes available.
Temperature cycles → structural-health data becomes available.

Tiny contributions individually.

Everywhere. All the time.

That's the interesting part.


---

02 — AMBIENTFORGE

Interior / never intentionally sun-exposed

This one gets a completely different optimization target.

No need to spend engineering budget defending the outer surface against decades of direct UV.

Instead:

human vibration → footsteps → doors → machinery → HVAC → plumbing → acoustic vibration → thermal gradients → electromagnetic/electrical infrastructure → structural strain

become the playground.

And because it's indoors, we can potentially make the member much more aggressive about embedded instrumentation.

Imagine a floor system where the structural members know:

> Something is vibrating unusually here.



Or a building where repeated foot traffic generates tiny harvested electrical pulses while simultaneously providing occupancy/activity information.

Or HVAC ducting and structural framing where temperature gradients become measurable.

Again, not pretending we're going to power the building with footsteps. 😂

The value is distributed micro-harvesting + distributed sensing, using infrastructure that was already going to exist.


